<br/>
For most buyers, the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; or &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max is the better choice. They offer the same A20 Pro chip as the Duo, a more versatile camera system, and a lower price. Choose the Pro if you prefer a smaller, lighter phone, or pay &#36;100 more for the Pro Max if you want a larger screen and longer battery life.<br/>
<br/>
The camera system is the clearest reason to choose an &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; over the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;. Alongside a more advanced Main camera, the Pro models offer a variable aperture for greater control over depth of field and a dedicated Telephoto camera for subjects you cannot get close to. These advantages matter even if you never use professional capture formats: The Pro gives you more flexibility both when framing a shot and when controlling how it looks. If photography or videography is a priority, choose the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj;. Buyers who also use ProRAW, ProRes, or manual controls have even more reason to do so.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; is worth considering if you frequently wish your phone had a larger screen for reading, documents, taking notes, or watching video. Its 7.6-inch folding display and &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; support are unique in the iPhone lineup and could reduce the need to carry a separate tablet. If you would unfold it several times a day for those sorts of tasks, the benefit may outweigh the camera compromises and higher price. If most of your use involves messaging, social media, and taking photos, there is less reason to pay extra for the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
The compromises also extend beyond the rear cameras. Both of its front-facing cameras are also inferior. The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; also replaces &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj; with &zwnj;Touch ID&zwnj; and drops the Action button, so buyers will need to adjust. It also lacks the Pro models' LiDAR Scanner, adaptive True Tone flash, four studio-quality microphones, and spatial photo and video recording.<br/>
<br/>
You also need to be comfortable with the Duo when it is closed. Despite its smaller outer screen, it is wider and thicker than either Pro model and slightly heavier than the Pro Max. Battery life is another reason to favor the Pro Max: The Duo matches the smaller Pro's rated typical-use battery life, and using its inner display reduces its video playback time substantially.<br/>
<br/>
The price gap remains significant at every storage tier. A 1TB &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max is only &#36;100 cheaper than the entry-level &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, but that Duo has just 256GB of storage. At the same capacity, you pay &#36;700 more than the Pro Max or &#36;800 more than the Pro. That premium needs to be justified by how much you would use the folding screen and how much you care about some of the device's compromises.<br/>
<br/>
Buy the Duo if you have a clear idea of the things you would regularly unfold it to do, or if experiencing Apple's first foldable and a radically new kind of iPhone is itself worth the premium to you. If you are unsure how the larger screen would fit into your routine, the Pro models remain the stronger purchase with their traditional "slab" form factor and host of superior specifications.<br/>
<br/>
